 #:seon.task{:name "an-edit-rewrites-every-definition-below-it"
             :intent "A publication rewrites `:seon.fn/form-span` and `:seon.program/analyzed-source-digest` for every definition whose lines moved, not for the ones whose source changed: analyze-forms stamps each definition with its whole FILE's digest (`:seon.fn.file/digest` of the analyzed source) and same-indexed-entity? only skips a definition when everything but that digest is equal, so a moved span rewrites both. On default's history (2026-10-07, db-watch lane) one publish rewrote 2,674 form-span/digest datoms for 136 changed `:seon.fn/source` datoms (20x), another 2,892 for 327; overall 19.5k of the 162k history-only datoms on default are these two attributes. Each rewritten definition touches its aevt/avet leaves, so the files written follow the file length below the edit. Smallest fix: the file digest lives once on the file entity (`:seon.fn.file/digest`), not copied per definition; keep form-span but compare it as position, not as a change of the definition. Done when editing one defn in a 100-definition file writes O(changed definitions) program datoms (measured with watch.clj :since-basis), with a regression on that count. Read `seon.fn/analyze-forms`, `seon.fn/same-indexed-entity?` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/db-watch/findings.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "backticked-docstring-references-have-no-index",
            :intent
            "CLAUDE.md says a backticked fully qualified symbol or keyword in a docstring is a reference the index resolves into the subject's context, one resolving to nothing a problem on that var. No producer exists: seon.fn indexes :seon/tasks, :seon/must-read and :seon/see-also metadata (task-links) and analyzer `:seon.fn/references`, never docstring spans. Done when docstring backtick spans are indexed as reference edges with dangling ones reported, or the law is corrected. Read `seon.fn/task-links` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/context-design/reference-kinds-notes.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "batched-analysis-uses-one-namespace-row-per-run"
  :intent "`seon.fn/runtime-analysis-batch` builds one ns form from the first row of a run of consecutive requests (partition-by on namespace name), so later requests with a different row are analyzed under the first. Probe (JVM, default's database value): two requests in namespace probe.a, row 1 x -> clojure.string, row 2 x -> clojure.set, forms (x/upper-case \"a\") and (x/union #{1} #{2}). Batched: usages [clojure.string upper-case] and [clojure.string union] (the second is wrong); analyzed one by one: [clojure.string upper-case] and [clojure.set union]. Correct: a run splits where the namespace row changes (not only the name). Proof: seon.fn-test with those two requests asserts [clojure.string upper-case] and [clojure.set union]. Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/s5-admission/alias-design.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-renamed-refer-drops-its-call-edge"
  :intent "runtime-require-specs (fn.clj:814) emits :refer [target-name] only and drops the local name of a :rename, so a renamed refer is invisible to analysis. Probe (probe.alias7): (require '[clojure.set :refer [union] :rename {union upper-case}]) then (defn r2 [] (upper-case ...)) stores calls [defn] with no edge to clojure.set/union. Correct: the analysis ns form carries :rename so the call resolves to clojure.set/union. Proof: that pair of forms stores clojure.set/union in r2's calls. Read `seon.fn/runtime-require-specs` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/s5-admission/alias-design.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "private-flag-disagrees-with-the-var-for-computed-metadata"
  :intent "(defn ^{:private (> 1 2)} falsy-meta [] 1) has Var :private false while the stored row says `:seon.fn/private?` true (fn.clj:746 reads clj-kondo's flag); the reverse holds for alter-meta! after the defn. Low severity. Correct: the row's private flag agrees with the Var's, or the source-derived privacy is stated as the rule. Proof: that form stores private? false. Read `seon.fn/runtime-analysis-batch` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/s5-admission/private-design.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-dependent-keeps-no-edge-to-a-callee-defined-after-it"
  :intent "(defn bad1 [x] (nosuch-fn x)) then (defn dep1 [x] (bad1 x)), then in a later pass a working bad1; and (declare later) (defn early [] (later)) (defn later [] 1): early and dep1 keep calls [clojure.core/defn] and gain no edge when the callee is defined. Related: seon.turn.batch/a-declared-var-call-stores-no-edge (the declare half). Correct: a caller's edge to a callee appears once the callee has an entity, without re-evaluating the caller. Proof: the last table row of the evidence (probe 7). Read `seon.fn/runtime-analysis-batch` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/s5-admission/failed-def-design.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "contract-facts-row-names-no-schema-in-its-refusal"
