 #:seon.task{:name "gate-set-walks-the-whole-transitive-closure-to-select-two-levels"
             :intent "`seon.fn/gate-set` answers `seon.fn/closure-walk`'s `::selected` (the tests calling a seed or a direct caller) but `seon.fn/closure-walk` first walks every transitive caller level and derives `::affected` and `::tests` the caller drops. Measured at default fa92a2503 (agent ref-context, 2026-10-09, `seon.profile/measure` of `seon.ref/uses`): `seon.fs/attributes` (2 callers, 1 selected test) takes 22 ms, 40 MB and 209 reads (183 `seon.db/datoms`); `seon.db/pull` takes 98 ms, 192 MB and 1265 reads. `seon.ref/references` now calls it for every referenced program function in a task's block. Done when a selection-only caller walks two levels, so a leaf's cost follows its callers, measured on the same two seeds as a regression on reads."} {:seon.task/name "a-file-proof-two-compiled-callers-away-never-sees-the-branch-redefinition", :seon.task/intent "`seon.fn/reverse-closure` now includes tests at every caller depth, and its branch-execution proof passes. Automatic selection through `seon.test/reaching` and `seon.fn/gate-sets` still selects tests of the seed and direct callers only; `seon.fn-test/tests-reaching-follows-calls-and-explicit-subjects` explicitly asserts that a distant test is omitted from selection. The original completion claim also required finding reaching tests at any depth, which these green proofs do not establish. Reconcile that requirement with the one-caller-level selection ruling before assigning this task: preserve correct branch interpretation, identify which distant tests must run for a changed function, and prove selection and actual branch execution together without whole-program saturation. Read `seon.sci.eval/interpret-overlay!` and `seon.test/resolve-test`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-finding-without-a-row-throws-in-lint-entities"
